{"episodes":{"179162":"The combination of a sea breeze and weak mid-level low combined to spark several showers and thunderstorms the evening of May 29th. Initially, the sea breeze produced a couple of showers and thunderstorms across Cameron, Kenedy, and Willacy Counties. As a weak mid-level low dropped south, it ran into the outflow boundaries of the sea breeze and ignited several storms across Hidalgo, Brooks, Jim Hogg, and Starr Counties. The largest hail fell over Hidalgo in southern Hidalgo County, where two inch hail was observed. Ping-pong ball sized hail was observed near Encino in Brooks County. There were a number of reports of pea to dime sized hail across southern Hidalgo County, including in and around McAllen.","179317":"A thunderstorm developed late in the afternoon on the 30th along the North Laramie Range and foothills with strong winds behind an initial line of storms that developed earlier in the day. Also received reports of funnel clouds in the Douglas area from this storm.","179383":"A thunderstorm briefly became severe over northern Tripp County, producing hail to quarter size.","179387":"A thunderstorm briefly became severe south of Rapid City and produced hail to quarter size.","179389":"A severe thunderstorm developed south of Rapid City and tracked east across southeastern Pennington County. The storm produced hail to golf ball size.","179391":"A severe thunderstorm developed near Hermosa and tracked east-southeast through northern Oglala Lakota County. The storm produced large hail along its path.","179392":"A thunderstorm briefly became severe in southeastern Lawrence County, bringing quarter sized hail northwest of Nemo.","179429":"A line of storms developed in northeast South Dakota on the early afternoon of May 30th.  The strongest of the storms produced a wind gust of 60 mph at the South Dakota Department of Transportation Mesonet station 4 miles north of Altamont. Additionally, a trained spotter 6 miles north of Gary reported that the combination of wind and hail damaged beans crops and the plants in his garden.","179459":"A weak shortwave passed across the area, coincident with a low level plume of moisture. Moderate instability was in place with CAPE around 1500 J/kg but very weak flow and shear. While this meant that storms generally remained sub severe, the storms were also slow moving. One storm that set up over Fort Doge resulted in heavy rain with over three inches observed in one hour. Flooding occurred throughout the town with several road closures and a few stranded vehicles.","180249":"Several severe storms produced large hail and damaging wind across Minnesota in the afternoon and evening hours of May 30th.","180377":"A line of thunderstorms moved across parts of central and eastern Minnesota on the evening of May 30th. Several special weather statements were issued through the course of the afternoon with potential threats of gusty winds from 30-40 mph and hail up to the size of nickels. Only one report of penny size hail was reported one mile SSE of Lester Prairie, MN.","180479":"On the night of May 29th, a line of thunderstorms moved across Northwestern Kansas. These thunderstorms produced an isolated wind gust of an estimated 70 mph in the region that caused a 12-inch long tree limb to break off a tree.","180542":"On the evening of May 30th, a line of thunderstorms developed in East-Central Colorado that moved across the region. Some of these thunderstorms produced hail up to quarters in size. Sub-severe thunderstorm wind gusts up to 54 mph were also observed along with heavy rainfall that yielded up to 4 inches of storm total precipitation during the event.","180575":"A cold front approached western and north central Nebraska on May 29th, initiating numerous thunderstorms across western and north central Nebraska. Some storms became severe, especially over the Sandhills. Hail up to quarter size, thunderstorm wind gusts up to 75 MPH and localized flooding was reported.","180636":"Storms developed ahead of a cold front across the region during the afternoon hours of the 30th. Conditions were marginal for severe weather, but a few storms in the Leech Lake area were able to produce hail of nickel to quarter size briefly before weakening.","180661":"Scattered thunderstorms produced severe hail and damaging winds during the afternoon of 5/30 in eastern Kansas.","180718":"A broad upper trough set the stage for a round of scattered showers and thunderstorms the afternoon of Tuesday May 30. One storm in Collin County contained frequent to continuous lightning. Multiple homes were hit by cloud to ground strikes, and a few of these homes sustained fire damage.","180841":"Storms produced large hail and damaging winds across portions of southwest Kansas.","180878":"An upper level shortwave trough generated thunderstorms in a very moist airmass. Some of these storms produced locally heavy rain that led to flash flooding.","180957":"A weak cold front sparked a few thunderstorms during the evening across southwest Minnesota, which strengthened just enough to produce isolated large hail near Jackson.","181067":"Numerous thunderstorms developed or moved into East Central Colorado during the evening.  Hail up to quarter size was reported with a couple of these storms.","181068":"Widespread thunderstorm activity occurred over Northwest Kansas during the evening.  Hail up to golf ball size was reported at Rexford.  Thunderstorm winds moving out ahead of the storm over Cheyenne County caused a localized dust storm on Highway 27.  Later in the evening wind gusts up to 59 MPH were reported near Norton.  The repeated rounds of rainfall over part of the area led to flooding in western Wallace County.","181338":"A weak frontal boundary on the afternoon of May 30th triggered a complex of strong to severe thunderstorms across portions of central and south-central Kansas. Storms produced up to quarter-sized hail across Marion, Chase, and Harvey counties.","181343":"Ample low level moisture and instability created another round of severe weather and landspout tornadoes."},"events":[{"begin":"2023-05-30T00:00Z","county":"Thomas","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2023-05-30T00:00Z","endLat":41.98,"endLon":-100.59,"episode":180575,"id":1099863,"injuries":0,"lat":41.98,"location":"0.51 W Thedford","lon":-100.59,"magnitude":1.0,"source":"Law Enforcement","state":"NE","type":"Hail","wfo":"LBF"},{"begin":"2023-05-30T00:08Z","county":"Thomas","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2023-05-30T00:08Z","endLat":41.98,"endLon":-100.57,"episode":180575,"id":1099864,"injuries":0,"lat":41.98,"location":"0.51 E Thedford","lon":-100.57,"magnitude":1.0,"source":"Trained Spotter","state":"NE","type":"Hail","wfo":"LBF"},{"begin":"2023-05-30T00:10Z","county":"Jackson","deaths":0,"end":"2023-05-30T00:20Z","endLat":43.62,"endLon":-94.99,"episode":180957,"id":1102032,"injuries":0,"lat":43.62,"location":"0.5 E Jackson","lon":-94.99,"magnitude":1.0,"narrative":"Occasional hail fell over a long period, at times reaching as large as penny to quarter size.","source":"Social Media","state":"MN","type":"Hail","wfo":"FSD"},{"begin":"2023-05-30T00:30Z","county":"Hidalgo","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2023-05-30T00:35Z","endLat":26.21,"endLon":-98.29,"episode":179162,"id":1090888,"injuries":0,"lat":26.21,"location":"0.93 SW Shayland","lon":-98.29,"magnitude":0.88,"narrative":"A member of the public called to report dime and nickel sized hail. Time estimated on the time of the call and radar.","source":"Public","state":"TX","type":"Hail","wfo":"BRO"},{"begin":"2023-05-30T00:36Z","county":"Bexar","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2023-05-30T02:30Z","endLat":29.4227,"endLon":-98.4911,"episode":180878,"id":1102954,"injuries":0,"lat":29.4238,"location":"0.27 NNW San Antonio","lon":-98.4911,"narrative":"Thunderstorms produced heavy rain that led 